implementationPlan.description:
TESTS FIRST, in the existing focused test packages/runner/src/providers/telemetry/claude-telemetry-launch.test.ts (526 lines; extend it, do not create a second file — task rail 1 names this file specifically).
Drive the factory through a REAL `createClaudeLauncher(authority)` by supplying only an authority. Reuse `authorityOf(grants, regs)` from packages/runner/src/providers/claude/claude-launcher-authority-test-fixtures.ts — importing that fixture is fine; rail 1 restricts what may be MODIFIED, not what may be imported.
Write RED:
- the factory accepts exactly one argument and returns an invocable; assert `fn.length === 1` so a later deps/launcher parameter cannot be added unnoticed;
- ONE-LAUNCH ORDERING: a counting authority records `consumeGrantDurably` calls; one invocation that reaches grant consumption yields a count of exactly 1. Counting through the AUTHORITY is deliberate — rail 2 forbids exposing a supplied-launcher hook, so the authority ports are the only honest observation point for "how many physical launches happened";
- RAW-RESULT IDENTITY: the success arm carries the launcher's own `ClaudeLaunchResult`, and the handoff was derived from THAT result — assert `bound.handoff.launch.kind === bound.result.kind` and, on the OBSERVED arm, that `handoff.launch.observationDigest`, `startedAt`, `completedAt` and `exit` equal the corresponding fields of `bound.result.observation`. Never compare against hand-written literals;
- the returned object is deeply frozen: `Object.isFrozen(bound)` and `Object.isFrozen(bound.result)` and `Object.isFrozen(bound.handoff.launch)`.
Confirm the suite fails because the symbol does not exist, not because the fixture is wrong.
